WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.760
Good morning John!

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:02.440
About a week ago I saw this comic

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:05.900
from Korean artist Yaho on Tumblr. It had 88,000 notes.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:10.240
This is, I feel, a common and persistent theme that I'm hearing from people on the internet.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.640
I heard it from people who came to my shows and talked to me.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.060
I hear it from people in my real life and in my own brain I hear this.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:20.980
The thing that we want and we crave in our lifes, in additon to stability, is meaning.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:22.900
And I think it's getting harder and harder to find.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.520
The whisper of culture it tells us that salary is a good surrogate for meaning.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:31.100
But we're also asks and we want to make a change to help people, to make the world a better place.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.520
We wanna have an impact. A big one like what's his face from Facebook?

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:35.380
Or Elon Musk!

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:36.900
I honestly can't remember his name right now.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.260
What is it? Zuckerberg!

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.340
And while you're at it might as well fulfill all the #goals.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.320
You gotta have your squad goals, and your relationship goals, and your career goals and your kitchen goals!

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:50.880
And if you don't have it all then you're not quite there. So keep working and keep wanting and someday, maybe.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.880
Culture has always told us lots of lies, but it seems like it's getting better at it.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:00.300
Look back at that comic. What signal are we getting from culture that individial humans matter at all?

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:06.560
When human labor is commoditized, as it is now, when do we stop appreciating the work that people do

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:07.820
to make our lives better?

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:12.380
And when that happens when do we stop ever feeling appreciated for the work that we do?

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:17.420
Without humans appreciating humans for the work they do where is meaning supposed to come from?

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:21.040
Earlier this week I asked Twitter if they would rather be happy, or make a difference.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:28.360
Now in addition to it being almost exactly a 50/50 split a ton of people said they could not imagine being happy

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.000
without making a difference.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.540
The problem is, our cultural mythology is the thing that tells us what counts as making a difference.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.220
And the most common surrogates for that are fame and money.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:38.820
And those metrics suck!

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.480
They have to suck, because they will always be scarce

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.120
And they will always be defined in relation to what other people have

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:45.560
not in absolute terms.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.800
We're headed into a world that is dramatically different from the one that our parents lived in.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.360
A world where many machines are going to take over the jobs that humans have had.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.400
And as fewer and fewer people have what we would consider normal employment, normal jobs.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.040
We're gonna have to redefine, not just what work is, but what meaning is.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.560
Because we all wanna make a difference and it makes sense that we all wanna make a difference

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.960
because we are a cultural species!

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:09.160
That drive is what has made humanity so wonderful and powerful.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:13.080
But I think we're starting to expect a little too much in this regard.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:18.960
We can't all be Elon Musk. In fact I would argue that Elon Musk can't be what we think of as Elon Musk.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.380
Thomas Edison didn't invent the light bulb. The Wright Brothers didn't invent flight.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.640
Elon Musk has never built an electric car.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.240
These are all stories. They are good stories they're inspiring stories, but they're stories we tell because

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.360
the real story is too complicated.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.240
That's the story of how every human being that has existed

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.080
and currently exists laboured together to build this world.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.200
And yeah, humans will always have arguments about the best way to do that and some of the ideas we've had

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.120
have been colossally bad.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:48.360
But in our tour van last week Joe DeGeorge said something to me that I will never let go of.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:57.360
Because we do. Every day and every night the good things that we have in our lives are mostly because

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.000
other people worked to make life better.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.480
And all I can ask is that my forgotten labours will add to the forgotten labours of other people

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.920
that have made life on earth better.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.600
And as I've worked harder to try and appreciate all of the work that has gone into building

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.920
my home and my town and my country and my world.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.240
I find myself being happier with who I am and where I am.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:23.800
Cause in the end we matter because we make life better for people around us

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.520
and also for people in the future.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:31.960
The story of humanity is not a story of a few people who had huge gigantic effects on the world.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:35.360
That's only the story we hear, because it's the easy story to tell.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.480
Caring for ourselves and other people is the only thing that has ever mattered to the future of our species.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:43.400
And sometimes even that care is very difficult,

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:44.400
but other times it won't be.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:50.120
And that, even when I've had really dark times in my life, is the one thing that I've tried to be sure about.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.720
John, I'll see you on Wednesday.

